WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.901 --> 00:00:14.566
No ever said married life would be easy, but Meg did not expect it to be so challenging.

2
00:00:15.546 --> 00:00:20.689
John was a kind, loving husband who helped Meg in every way possible, for which she was truly

3
00:00:20.749 --> 00:00:26.431
grateful. Still, Meg found the little chores in everyday life that she had taken for granted at home

4
00:00:26.491 --> 00:00:28.032
were now a huge challenge for her.

5
00:00:29.056 --> 00:00:33.359
Then of course there were new issues that Meg had never experienced at home but had to learn

6
00:00:33.399 --> 00:00:34.700
to deal with as a married woman.

7
00:00:36.121 --> 00:00:41.245
One day, as she was doing the simple act of preparing a meal, Meg felt dizzy and sick

8
00:00:41.265 --> 00:00:41.885
to her stomach.

9
00:00:42.586 --> 00:00:46.229
Fortunately, John was in the kitchen at the time and carried her to the sofa.

10
00:00:46.249 --> 00:00:49.011
"Can I bring you anything?

11
00:00:49.491 --> 00:00:50.672
Water? A blanket?"

12
00:00:51.032 --> 00:00:52.513
he asked in a concerned voice.

13
00:00:52.533 --> 00:00:56.136
"Just some hot tea, thank you," Meg said faintly.

14
00:00:57.140 --> 00:01:00.042
By the time John brought the drink to her, Meg had gotten worse.

15
00:01:00.062 --> 00:01:04.305
"Darling, I feel very ill," she told her husband.

16
00:01:05.146 --> 00:01:06.107
"Shall I fetch Dr.

17
00:01:06.127 --> 00:01:08.248
Bangs?" he asked, squeezing her hand tightly.

18
00:01:08.268 --> 00:01:10.670
"No," Meg said.

19
00:01:10.690 --> 00:01:12.071
"I need my mother."

20
00:01:13.072 --> 00:01:17.054
John raced out of the house and down the street to get his mother-in-law, who, along with Beth,

21
00:01:17.795 --> 00:01:22.018
was in the process of giving each window in the house a good shine before the winter set

22
00:01:22.098 --> 00:01:23.499
in. "John!

23
00:01:23.939 --> 00:01:24.640
What's the matter?"

24
00:01:25.024 --> 00:01:27.046
Mother asked him when she saw his worried look.

25
00:01:27.066 --> 00:01:30.328
"It's Meg," he said, gasping for breath.

26
00:01:30.769 --> 00:01:33.131
"She's not well and I don't know what to do."

27
00:01:34.172 --> 00:01:39.336
Throwing down her cloth, Mother wiped her hands and said, "Let's not waste another moment while the poor

28
00:01:39.396 --> 00:01:40.237
girl lies sick."

29
00:01:41.017 --> 00:01:44.920
When they got to Meg and John's house, Meg was sweating and looking very pale.

30
00:01:46.261 --> 00:01:50.025
Mother put her hand on Meg's forehead, over her heart, then down on her stomach.

31
00:01:50.385 --> 00:01:52.006
Then, surprisingly, Mother smiled.

32
00:01:52.026 --> 00:01:53.988
"What's the matter with her?"

33
00:01:54.320 --> 00:01:55.661
John asked, his voice trembling.

34
00:01:55.681 --> 00:01:57.503
"Is Meg going to be all right?"

35
00:01:57.523 --> 00:02:01.286
"She's going to be fine," Mother replied, nodding.

36
00:02:01.306 --> 00:02:04.489
"Meg isn't sick—she's pregnant."

37
00:02:05.649 --> 00:02:10.694
Upon hearing those words, John collapsed into a heap on the floor next to his wife.

38
00:02:12.135 --> 00:02:17.399
Meanwhile, Jo, who was not yet aware that she was soon going to become an aunt, spent her

39
00:02:17.439 --> 00:02:21.703
days content to be a governess and her evenings very happy to be with a friend.

40
00:02:21.723 --> 00:02:28.986
"Jo, this week you must try to read this thrilling adventure that will take you across the seas,"

41
00:02:29.587 --> 00:02:33.648
the Professor said, handing Jo a thick, old book from his own collection.

42
00:02:34.489 --> 00:02:39.211
Since their first encounter a couple of weeks ago, Jo found that she had a lot in common

43
00:02:39.251 --> 00:02:40.011
with the Professor.

44
00:02:40.851 --> 00:02:46.213
They both loved to read and write, though she preferred writing fictional stories and he favored poetry.

45
00:02:47.494 --> 00:02:52.952
When he had moved to America, the Professor was forced to leave behind many of his possessions but

46
00:02:52.972 --> 00:02:57.935
he could not bear to part with his beloved collection of books, which he now displayed proudly in

47
00:02:57.975 --> 00:02:58.956
his personal library.

48
00:02:59.837 --> 00:03:05.620
Jo considered it an honor that the Professor wanted her to read some of his dearest treasures (though

49
00:03:05.660 --> 00:03:09.583
at first when he offered her his favorite book, she did not have the heart to tell him

50
00:03:09.623 --> 00:03:10.984
that she could not read German).

51
00:03:11.524 --> 00:03:17.408
Fortunately, the Professor owned a series of books translated into English that Jo was able to enjoy thoroughly.

52
00:03:19.216 --> 00:03:23.279
On the weekends, the Professor would invite Jo to go with him and his nephews to the park

53
00:03:23.899 --> 00:03:28.722
or to take a carriage to Coney Island, an amusement park where children (of all ages) could go

54
00:03:28.842 --> 00:03:29.362
on rides.

55
00:03:30.423 --> 00:03:34.985
Jo noticed that the more time she spent with the Professor, the broader her horizon became.

56
00:03:35.746 --> 00:03:38.427
There seemed to be no end to the amount of knowledge to be learned.

57
00:03:39.428 --> 00:03:44.551
Because the Professor was teaching her so many new things, Jo wanted to show her thanks but she

58
00:03:44.691 --> 00:03:46.152
couldn't find a way to do so.

59
00:03:46.964 --> 00:03:50.686
Then one evening, the opportunity arose as she was washing her clothes.

60
00:03:52.046 --> 00:03:57.128
She happened to find two pairs of his socks, which he had probably dropped without realizing, on the

61
00:03:57.168 --> 00:03:58.169
floor of the laundry room.

62
00:03:59.229 --> 00:04:04.112
They were still damp and as Jo laid them out to dry, she noticed they all had holes

63
00:04:04.152 --> 00:04:04.412
in them.

64
00:04:05.472 --> 00:04:10.114
Smiling to herself as she recalled the first time she met him, Jo was about to leave when

65
00:04:10.154 --> 00:04:14.196
it occurred to her that perhaps she could help the Professor by mending his holey socks.

66
00:04:15.644 --> 00:04:19.687
"I'll bring them back here after I do it so he won't know it was me," Jo thought

67
00:04:19.707 --> 00:04:20.207
to herself.

68
00:04:20.788 --> 00:04:25.611
Putting the socks into her laundry basket, she quickly climbed the stairs to her room and took out

69
00:04:25.651 --> 00:04:26.312
her sewing kit.

70
00:04:27.533 --> 00:04:32.416
While Meg had just found out she was soon to be a mother and Jo was experiencing feelings

71
00:04:32.516 --> 00:04:37.320
she had never felt before, Amy was also discovering something new about herself.

72
00:04:38.240 --> 00:04:43.144
She sensed that any day now, Fred was going to propose to her and she was prepared.

73
00:04:44.264 --> 00:04:48.968
Sure enough, one chilly evening, Fred made an unannounced visit to Amy and her aunt.

74
00:04:48.988 --> 00:04:56.634
"Why, Fred," Aunt March said fondly, for she was sure that he would be a perfect match for

75
00:04:56.694 --> 00:04:57.434
her grandniece.

76
00:04:58.215 --> 00:05:00.136
"What a pleasant surprise!

77
00:05:00.457 --> 00:05:02.318
To what do we owe this honor?"

78
00:05:03.119 --> 00:05:09.183
Amy sat quietly clasping her trembling hands while she waited for the now inescapable question to fall from

79
00:05:09.263 --> 00:05:09.824
Fred's lips.

80
00:05:11.024 --> 00:05:16.065
As he spoke to Aunt March, revealing his love for Amy, she watched him, feeling horrible about what

81
00:05:16.106 --> 00:05:19.867
she was going to do but at the same time knowing it was the right thing.

82
00:05:21.127 --> 00:05:26.829
Before she knew it, Fred was on bended knee asking, "Amy March, will you be my wife?"

83
00:05:27.989 --> 00:05:32.210
Aunt March relaxed in her chair, absolutely certain what Amy's answer would be.

84
00:05:32.230 --> 00:05:37.572
"I'm sorry, Fred, I can't marry you," Amy said clearly.

85
00:05:37.592 --> 00:05:41.254
"What?" Aunt March's eyes bulged in shock.

86
00:05:42.134 --> 00:05:43.955
"Amy, did you hear what Fred said?

87
00:05:44.436 --> 00:05:45.637
He wants to marry you!"

88
00:05:45.657 --> 00:05:51.180
"I know, Aunt March, but I don't want to marry him," Amy said respectfully.

89
00:05:51.200 --> 00:05:52.681
"But why not?"

90
00:05:53.082 --> 00:05:54.102
demanded the old woman.

91
00:05:55.003 --> 00:05:58.565
Poor Fred was in a state of such shock that he was speechless.

92
00:05:59.406 --> 00:06:02.188
"Because I don't love him," Amy admitted honestly.

93
00:06:02.208 --> 00:06:06.231
"And I must marry for love or I won't be truly happy.

94
00:06:06.831 --> 00:06:07.712
I know that now."

95
00:06:08.772 --> 00:06:13.737
If only Jo could have heard Amy's admission at that moment, she would have marveled at how her

96
00:06:13.797 --> 00:06:14.918
sister had changed.

97
00:06:16.099 --> 00:06:21.465
And if Amy, for her part, could have seen Jo secretly smiling as she tenderly placed the mended

98
00:06:21.525 --> 00:06:26.570
socks back in the laundry room, Amy would have been shocked at the difference in her sister as

99
00:06:26.610 --> 00:06:32.675
well. Winter was coming to an end, which meant that this chapter of Jo's journey would conclude shortly

100
00:06:32.715 --> 00:06:38.925
too. Jo's stay in New York had exceeded all her expectations and more as she looked back on

101
00:06:38.965 --> 00:06:40.487
her six months away from home.

102
00:06:41.789 --> 00:06:47.895
Feeling stronger, more mature, as well as more fulfilled, Jo readied herself to make the trip home.

103
00:06:49.377 --> 00:06:53.522
At the same time, she felt a dull ache in her heart because that meant she would have

104
00:06:53.562 --> 00:06:55.404
to say good-bye to a dear friend.
